==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|existence}}, from {{der|en|fro|existence}}, from {{der|en|LL.|existentia||existence}}, from {{m|la|existēns}}, from {{m|la|exsisto|existō, exsistō|I am, I exist}}, from {{m|la|ex||out}} + {{m|la|sisto|sistere|to set, place}} (related to {{m|la|stō|stare|to stand, to be stood}}), ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*stísteh₂ti}}, from the root {{m|ine-pro|*steh₂-||stand}}. Cognate with {{cog|es|existencia}}, {{cog|fr|existence}}, {{cog|de|Existenz}}.

Morphologically {{suffix|en|exist|ence|nocat=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɛɡˈzɪs.təns/|/ɪɡˈzɪs.təns/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-existence.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The state of [[being]], [[exist]]ing, or [[occur]]ring; [[beinghood]].
#: {{syn|en|presence}}
#: {{ux|en|In order to destroy evil, we must first acknowledge its '''existence'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|page=71|volume=II|text=Fortunate it is for the tranquillity of the new-born infant, if he have any turn for philosophy, that he understands none of the nonsense consecrated by old usage to the commencement of '''existence'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=1|passage=However, with the dainty volume my quondam friend sprang into fame. At the same time he cast off the '''chrysalis''' of a commonplace '''existence'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2012|month=March-April|author=w:Jeremy Bernstein
|volume=100|issue=2|page=146|magazine=w:American Scientist
|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2012/2/a-palette-of-particles A Palette of Particles]
|passage=The physics of elementary particles in the 20th century was distinguished by the observation of particles whose '''existence''' had been predicted by theorists sometimes decades earlier.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=June 29, 2020|author=Wendi|title=The Loyal General Yue Fei|magazine=Minghui|url=https://en.minghui.org/html/articles/2020/6/29/185686.html|text=The ancients said, “A ruler should exist for the '''existence''' of the people.” The famous thinker, Mencius noted, “The people are the most valuable, then the country, and the ruler comes last.”}}
# [[empirical|Empirical]] [[reality]]; the [[substance]] of the physical [[universe]]. (Dictionary of Philosophy; 1968)

